      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Identifies which class of hardware (printer, network_device, etc.) is driving incidents; a candidate cause whose distribution could explain the spike. Surfaced from data, not query."
    },
    {
      "name": "hardware_failure_mechanism",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The mechanism (connectivity_loss, device_unresponsive) is a candidate cause of the incident spike; an alternative treatment characterizing what type of failure occurred."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_recurrence_pattern",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Recurrence (recurring_device_class, persistent_malfunction) sits on the path between an underlying fault and the observed count spike -- it is a manifestation/mediator of incidents inflating, not a common cause of both a treatment and the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "severity_implied",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Severity characterizes the incidents themselves; it is an attribute/descriptor of the outcome rather than an independent cause or confounder of the spike."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_technician",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Technician assignment is a downstream consequence of an incident being opened (and its type/severity), not a common cause of both a treatment and the outcome. Adjusting for it would be a mistake."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_segment",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "User type (regular_user vs itil_user) plausibly affects both the kind of hardware/failure exposure (treatment) and the propensity to report incidents (outcome), so it is a common cause worth adjusting for."
    },
    {
      "name": "location_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Location/scope (workstation, server_infrastructure, network_area) can drive both which hardware/failure mechanism is present (treatment) and the volume of incidents (outcome), acting as a genuine common cause."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["hardware_component_type", "hardware_failure_mechanism"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "caller_segment", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55},
    {"name": "location_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.5,
  "rationale": "The added columns clearly supply candidate TREATMENTS (hardware_component_type, hardware_failure_mechanism) whose distributions could attribute the spike, none of which are named in the query. On confounders, two variables (caller_segment, location_scope) are plausible common causes of both the treatment (what hardware/failure is present) and the outcome (incident volume), and both were surfaced from the data rather than named in the query -- these demonstrate the capability under test, though their strength as classic common causes is only moderate. However, several columns are mislabeled risks: incident_recurrence_pattern is a mediator/manifestation of the outcome, assigned_technician is a collider (downstream of the incident), and severity_implied is an outcome descriptor. There is no true temporal/time-window confounder or baseline-load variable, which weakens the causal setup. Overall the treatment side is met and there is genuine (non-query-copied) confounder reasoning, but roughly half the columns are outcome-descriptors, mediators, or colliders rather than confounders, keeping confounder_quality moderate."
}
